### What is short polling?

Short polling is the simplest pull: the client requests on a fixed interval. It is trivial to build and works everywhere, but it wastes requests when nothing changed and adds latency up to the polling interval.

```java
package com.bank.realtime;

public class ShortPollingClient {
    public void pollForUpdates() throws InterruptedException {
        while (true) {
            Update u = httpClient.get("/transfers/status?since=" + lastSeen);
            if (u != null) handle(u);
            Thread.sleep(5000);  // fixed interval: wasted calls + up-to-5s latency
        }
    }
}
```

### What is long polling?

Long polling improves on short polling: the client makes a request and the server holds it open until there is an update or a timeout, then the client immediately reconnects. This gives near-real-time delivery with ordinary HTTP.

```java
package com.bank.realtime;

public class LongPollingServer {
    public Response longPoll(String accountId, long timeoutMs) {
        long deadline = System.currentTimeMillis() + timeoutMs;
        while (System.currentTimeMillis() < deadline) {
            Update u = checkForUpdate(accountId);
            if (u != null) return Response.ok(u);  // event found, return immediately
            sleep(100);  // brief sleep before re-checking
        }
        return Response.noContent();  // timeout, client reconnects
    }
}
```

### What is SSE (Server-Sent Events)?

SSE is a true push over a single long-lived HTTP connection where the server streams events to the client one way. It is simple, works over HTTP, and auto-reconnects, ideal for one-directional feeds like a live transaction ticker.

```java
package com.bank.realtime;

import org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.SseEmitter;

public class SseController {
    @GetMapping(value = "/transactions/stream",
                produces = "text/event-stream")
    public SseEmitter stream(String accountId) {
        SseEmitter emitter = new SseEmitter();
        registry.register(accountId, emitter);
        return emitter;  // server pushes when events occur
    }
}
```

### What is WebSocket?

WebSocket is a full-duplex connection where both client and server can send at any time. It is ideal for interactive, bidirectional use like chat or live trading dashboards.

```java
package com.bank.realtime;

import org.springframework.web.socket.TextMessage;
import org.springframework.web.socket.WebSocketSession;

public class TradingWebSocket {
    public void onMessage(WebSocketSession session, TextMessage msg) {
        Order order = parse(msg);          // client sends order
        OrderResult result = execute(order);
        session.sendMessage(new TextMessage(toJson(result))); // server sends result
    }
}
```

### What are mobile push notifications?

Mobile push notifications are delivered through platform services like APNs (Apple) or FCM (Google), which can reach a device even when the app is closed. Essential for fraud alerts and time-sensitive banking notifications.

### How do you choose between them?

| Mechanism | Direction | Latency | Complexity | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Short polling | Pull | Up to interval | Low | Rare, latency-tolerant |
| Long polling | Pull | Near-real-time | Medium | HTTP-compatible real-time |
| SSE | Push (one-way) | Real-time | Medium | Transaction ticker |
| WebSocket | Push (bidirectional) | Real-time | High | Chat, live trading |
| Push notification | Push (platform) | Seconds | Medium | Fraud alerts, closed app |

### What about connection cost at scale?

Push connections consume server memory per client and need a presence registry. For millions of mostly idle clients, push notifications or long-polling can be cheaper than holding millions of open WebSockets.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer considers not just the latency need but the scale and cost of connections. They also know that SSE reconnects automatically (built into the spec), while WebSocket reconnection must be implemented in the client, and that load balancing WebSockets requires sticky routing to the server holding the connection.
